<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prince-elector</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as electors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A prince-elector was one of the princes entitled to elect the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> fixed their number at seven: the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>. The electoral system institutionalised the elective character of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-elector.jpg" alt="Prince-elector" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balduineum_Wahl_Heinrich_VII.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The German practice of electing monarchs began when ancient Germanic tribes formed ad hoc coalitions and elected their leaders. While all free men originally voted, suffrage came to be limited to the leading men of the realm. At the election of Lothar III in 1125 a small number of eminent nobles chose the monarch and then submitted him to the remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> for approval, and soon the right to choose was settled on an exclusive group of princes. A letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv">Pope Urban IV</a> in 1265 suggests that by immemorial custom seven princes held the right, namely those who had voted in the disputed election of 1257, which produced two kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Disputes followed over who held the Wittelsbach seat, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a> or the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and over the right of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> to take part. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> settled the disputes. The composition then remained unchanged until the 17th century, except that in 1547, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> electorate passed from the Ernestine to the Albertine branch of the Wettin family.</p><p>In 1623 Frederick V, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, came under the imperial ban after the Bohemian Revolt and his seat went to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648 created a new post for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a>, raising the number to eight. A further post, for the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, was created in 1692. In 1777 the number fell back to eight when the Elector Palatine inherited Bavaria. In 1803 new electorates were created for Württemberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> was abolished in 1806 before any of the new electors could vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Electors ranked behind only the emperor, kings and the highest dukes, and held privileges not shared with other princes; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> gave them the Privilegium de non appellando, barring their subjects from appeal to a higher imperial court. Each held a high office of the Empire: the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> was Archchancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> a majority sufficed, each elector had one vote, and elections were held regularly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> from the 14th century. From the 16th century electors drafted a Wahlkapitulation, a contract that the king-elect swore to keep. All kings elected from 1438 to 1740 were from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>. After 1806 the former electors kept their lands, many taking the titles of king or grand duke, and Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> remained an electorate until 1866.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prince-elector” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the vineyards of the Haardt and the Rhine valley, and especially from the Rhine tolls collected at places such as Bacharach and Kaub, where the island toll castle of Pfalzgrafenstein still stands, which made the <b>electors</b> among the richest princes of the Empire in the later Middle Ages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…in 1356 settled their dispute over the right to elect the King of the Romans in favour of Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg, making him one of the seven prince-<b>electors</b> and Arch-Marshal of the Empire, with the lands around Wittenberg on the middle Elbe attached to the dignity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Golden Bull of 1356, issued by Emperor Charles IV, codified the election of the King of the Romans by seven designated <b>electors</b>: the archbishops of Mainz, Trier and Cologne, the King of Bohemia, the Count Palatine of the Rhine, the Duke of Saxony and the Margrave of Brandenburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles IV (r. 1346–1378), crowned emperor in 1355, made Prague the residence of the empire, obtained an archbishopric for Prague in 1344 and in the Golden Bull of 1356 confirmed the king of Bohemia as the first among the secular <b>electors</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Prussia was created from the lands that the Hohenzollern <b>electors</b> of Brandenburg had gathered over the previous century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1701 the army that the <b>electors</b> had built became the army of the Kingdom of Prussia, with which Frederick the Great conquered Silesia from 1740 and survived the Seven Years’ War of 1756–1763.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li></ul></section>
